  "updated": "2026-05-11T12:25:56.950939+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Frost Advisory/Freeze Warning for the NW Mtns \nthrough early today and a Freeze Watch for the Laurels, Central\nand Northern Mtns Monday night/early Tuesday. \n\nA several hour period of high clouds streaming NE across the NW\nhalf of PA have moved into upstate NY and New England as of 08Z\nand the combination of this clearing, light wind and low\ndewpoints (in the wake of yesterday's Cfropa) has caused temps\nto dip into the 30-32F range in numerous locations across the NW\nMtns with the lowest reading (so far) coming in at 29F in\nNorthern McKean Cty. This agrees well with the current Freeze \nWarning, while counties within this morning's frost advisory are\nseeing predawn temps in the mid-upper 30s.\n\nThere will be a higher frost/freeze risk for tonight/Tuesday \nmorning over the western and central Alleghenies. Dewpoints will\nbe 25-30F - just low enough to allow the temps to be able to \ndrop, but not too dry to keep frost away. \n\nConfidence in frost is near 100 pct, but for freeze is about 49\npct. Due to increasing confidence, frost advy and freeze \nwarnings seem likely, but will allow the current Freeze Warning/Frost\nAdvis to expire at 12Z before following shortly afterward with\nheadline transitions/upgrades.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Potent/amplifying, northern stream upper level \ntrough moves through for the Wed-Thu period with more widespread\nclouds, occasional showers/isolated low topped TSRA and maybe \neven a few wet snow flakes on the higher ridge tops of the \nLaurels and Northern Mtns (Thurs Morning and Night). Showers\nmove into the NW Mtns Tuesday night with the bulk of the rain\nstarting across the Central Mtns and Susq Valley around or after\n12Z Wed. \n\nA digging/compact upper low with colder temps aloft (and lower \n500 mb heights by 40-60 dam at its base at 12Z Thu) than the one\nmoving across the CWA today will drop across the Upper Great \nLakes Tuesday and bring SHRA into western PA by Wednesday AM. \n\nProbabilities for greater than 0.50 inch/24 hr exceed 60 Percent\nacross the far NW part of the CWA while QPF elsewhere (and centered\non Wed) will be between 0.25-0.40.\n\nWAA and MU CAPE doesn't look particularly strong in advance of \nthe system, so any TSRA should remain quite isolated and low-\ntopped with CAPE of 500 J/KG or less.\n\nLastly, the wet bulb zero level descends to under 2 KFT AGL\nacross the NW Mtns later Wed night into Thurs night (but is a\ntad higher the a few model cycles back). It's likely that \nsome wet snow flakes will be flying at that time (mainly 06-15Z\nThu and after 00Z Friday).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Warmup beginning this weekend into next week.\n\nNorthern stream relaxes/becomes more zonal and \nthicknesses/heights rebound for the weekend and become about 1 \nsigma above normal (standard anomaly-wise at 500 mb) by 12z \nMonday. \n\nAs this transition occurs over the weekend, moisture/energy \nflows over the flattening western/central U.S. upper ridge\nbringing the chance for a few bouts of light QPF from isolated\nto sctd SHRA/TSRA. \n\nUncertainty in details (esp precip possibilities) is high as \nthe spread in the mass fields over the weekend is rather high. \nEventually, a sfc high is expected to park over Bermuda and \ndirect warm/summery air in for next week (days 7+).",
